            {
                "box": {
                    "code": "uRound_maxDenom3(k)\r\n{\r\n    // we're assuming k>0 here, hence \"uRound\"\r\n    whole = floor(k);\r\n    fract = k - whole;\r\n    \r\n    // round the fractional part in  sixths\r\n    n = round(fract * 6); \r\n    \r\n    // after this if statment the possible values for the fractional part are:\r\n    // 0/6 == 0, 2/6 == 1/3, 3/6 == 1/2, 4/6 == 2/3\r\n    // this gives all the possible fractional values with max denom 3\r\n    // integers have a stronger \"attraction\" than other numbers\r\n    if (n == 1 || n == 5) n = 0;\r\n    \r\n    return whole + n/6;\r\n}\r\n\r\nuRound_maxDenom4(k)\r\n{\r\n    // we're assuming k>0 here, hence \"uRound\"\r\n    whole = floor(k);\r\n    fract = k - whole;\r\n    \r\n    // round fractional part in 12ths\r\n    n = round (fract * 12);  \r\n    \r\n    // after these two statements the possible values for the fractional part are:\r\n    // 0/12 == 0, 3/12 = 1/4, 4/12 = 1/3, 6/12 = 1/2, 8/12 = 2/3, 9/12 = 3/4\r\n    // this gives all the possible fractional values with max denom 4\r\n    // integers and half-integers have a stronger \"attraction\" than other numbers - \r\n    // with integers having the strongest pull of all.\r\n    if (n <= 2 || n >= 10) n = 0;\r\n    if (n == 5 || n == 7)  n = 6;\r\n    \r\n    return whole + n/12;\r\n}\r\n\r\n/* so we make a lil data object. 1-D with length len(buf_ratiosOfInterest)\r\ninitialise it to all -1's\r\nthen we iterate over buf_features (rounding Wr remember) and for \r\nech of those features we figure out which index in ratiosOfInterest they correspond to\r\n(this is the same as the eventual buf_smoothed_features index) \r\nthen we go and write in our lil data buffuer: buf[ROI_Idx] = Features_Idx\r\n(thats why we had to initialise it to -1s btw.)\r\n\r\nthen we iterate over buf_smoothedFeatures in strides of smoothedFeatureStride (maybe \r\nthis is the same as featureStride), and its smooth sailing from there on out\r\nwe just look in our little auxillary buffer and it tells us where to get the values to smooth towards \r\n(or we use a bunch of default values if its a -1 entry) \r\nand we do the smoothing based on a control input \r\n(so that we can have \"sustain pedal\" style interaction later)\r\nwe can do the unwrapping of tau and phi (lel lel lel) by just smoothing toward the possible phi/tau value thats closest \r\nmod 2 pi or pi or whatever the current vibe is\r\n\r\nthat handles the \"cycles\" input, what about the \"exponent\" input? \r\nwell... we do need to scale by the energy (AFTER smoothing) \r\n(soooo maybe the time has come to finally require euclidian params)\r\nmaybe we can actually steal a march by just taking an exponent on the energy \r\n(which after all is in [0..1] already) before we multiply through the params. \r\n\r\n",
                    "fontface": 0,
                    "fontname": "<Monospaced>",
                    "fontsize": 12.0,
